```
SYSTEM OVERVIEW
===============
The system is a Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) platform supporting multi-user operations with role hierarchy, temporary delegation, clearance-based authorization, and concurrent request processing. The system enforces security constraints such as clearance-based permissions, role delegation and revocation, and preserves safety, liveness, and integrity under concurrent operations. A new Emergency Module is to be integrated, enabling a global/system-wide operational mode and privileges during emergencies, with specific triggering, duration, and exit requirements. Emergency mode is a system-wide state: when Emergency mode is triggered, all active sessions enter Emergency mode together, and no session may remain in or independently return to Normal mode until the Emergency ends. Only one Emergency may be active at a time. Administrators may not initiate or participate in separate concurrent Emergencies or acquire independent emergency privileges through different sessions. Emergency privileges and policy relaxations apply consistently across all sessions and are revoked globally when the system returns to Normal mode. Emergency mode can be triggered exactly once per session by exactly 2 administrators, is active for an abstract fixed duration but may be ended early by the same 2 administrators, and grants special privileges only to the triggering administrators, applied globally across all sessions. While Emergency mode is active, new triggers are blocked. Roles delegated during Emergency mode are revoked upon exit (before the first time step of Normal mode), while roles delegated prior persist as per their policy.

EXISTING-SYSTEM ASSUMPTIONS AND CONSTRAINTS
============================================
- Only exactly 2 administrators may jointly trigger Emergency mode when the system is in Normal mode; each session may only trigger Emergency mode once. Emergency mode entry is permitted only under these conditions.
- Emergency mode is a global/system-wide state and is time-limited by an abstract fixed duration and cannot persist indefinitely; the system must ensure eventual return to Normal mode for all sessions together. Early termination is permitted by the same 2 administrators who triggered Emergency mode.
- For every EmergencyTrigger, endedAt must be set (by either timeout or early termination). The system MUST return to NormalMode for all sessions together no later than the endedAt of the EmergencyTrigger, and restoration must not be delayed past endedAt.
- No new Emergency mode triggers are accepted while Emergency mode is active.
- During Emergency mode, request processing order may be bypassed to prioritize high classification requests; this relaxation is temporary and applies system-wide.
- Users may temporarily hold predefined mutually exclusive roles only during Emergency mode. Mutual exclusivity must be symmetric (if A is mutex with B, then B is mutex with A) and paired, and roles cannot be mutex with themselves.
- In Emergency mode, if a user holds two mutually exclusive roles, at least one must have been delegated.
- Only the administrators who triggered Emergency mode hold emergency privileges, defined as an abstract set of permissions, and only for the duration of Emergency mode. Emergency privileges are assigned and revoked globally across all sessions.
- All roles delegated during Emergency mode must be revoked at the Emergency end, before the first time step of Normal mode. Roles delegated prior to Emergency mode persist until expiry or administrator revocation.
- Administrators who held emergency privileges are required to update their credentials immediately upon exit from Emergency mode before resuming normal operations.
- Self-delegation is prohibited: a user cannot delegate a role to themselves.
- Only directly assigned roles may be delegated; delegation of delegated roles is prohibited.
- Delegation authority is restricted to users with direct assignment of the role.
- The expiry of a delegated role must be strictly after its delegation time (delegatedAt).
- For every request, the request processor (the entity processing the request) must be recorded, regardless of whether the request was approved or rejected.
- All administrators have high clearance, but not all high-clearance users are administrators.

PROSPECTIVE FUNCTIONAL REQUIREMENTS
====================================
R1: Emergency Triggering  
Exactly 2 administrators, while the system is in Normal mode, are required to trigger an emergency, causing the system to enter Emergency mode globally. Each session may only trigger Emergency mode once, and no new emergency triggers are accepted while Emergency mode is active. Emergency mode may be triggered only by exactly two administrators under these conditions.

R2: Emergency Mode Duration  
For every EmergencyTrigger, the system MUST return to NormalMode for all sessions together no later than EmergencyTrigger.endedAt, and remain in NormalMode for all subsequent times. Emergency mode may end either by an abstract fixed duration (timeout) or by early termination by the same 2 administrators who triggered it. The return to NormalMode must occur at or before endedAt; restoration must not be delayed past endedAt.

R3: Modified Processing and Privileges During Emergency  
R3.1: During Emergency mode, request processing may bypass FIFO order to prioritize multiple high classification requests simultaneously, system-wide.  
R3.2: During Emergency mode, users may temporarily hold mutually exclusive roles (as defined in the set of mutually exclusive abstract roles). If a user holds two mutually exclusive roles during Emergency mode, at least one must have been delegated (mutex role bypass requirement).  
R3.3: During Emergency mode, only the administrators who triggered Emergency mode temporarily hold emergency privileges, defined as an abstract set of permissions available exclusively during Emergency mode. Emergency privileges are assigned and revoked globally across all sessions.

R4: Post-Emergency Restoration  
R4.1: Emergency privileges must be revoked globally for all triggering administrators at the end of Emergency mode, prior to the first time step of Normal mode; at no time in NormalMode may any administrator hold emergency privileges.  
R4.2: All delegations that are initiated during Emergency mode must be revoked before or at the Emergency/Normal transition (the first time step of Normal mode). Delegations initiated in Normal mode, even if active during Emergency, do not require revocation at this transition. Delegations made during Normal mode, before Emergency mode, can persist across Emergency mode, unless revoked by an admin or expired during Emergency. Delegations during Normal mode must continue until their expiry or revocation, regardless of the system's mode transition.  
R4.3: Administrators who held emergency privileges must update their credentials immediately after Emergency mode ends, before resuming normal operations.

R5: Delegation Requirements  
R5.1: A user cannot delegate a role to themselves (self-delegation prohibition).  
R5.2: Only directly assigned roles may be delegated; delegation of delegated roles is prohibited.  
R5.3: Delegation authority is restricted to users with direct assignment of the role.  
R5.4: The expiry of a delegated role must be strictly after its delegation time (delegatedAt).

R6: Mutual Exclusion (Mutex) Role Requirements  
R6.1: Mutually exclusive roles must be defined in pairs and mutual exclusivity must be symmetric: if Role A is mutex with Role B, then Role B is mutex with Role A.  
R6.2: A role cannot be mutually exclusive with itself.

R7: Request Processing Logging  
R7.1: For every request (regardless of outcome), the system must record the processor responsible for the request.

R8: Delegation Coverage Verification  
R8.1: For verification purposes only, it is sufficient to show that (a) there exists an execution with a delegation initiated and active during Emergency mode, and (b) there exists an execution with a delegation initiated and active during Normal mode; these do not need to occur in the same execution. The absence of a delegation in either mode is permitted in actual system operation.
