Previous Lessons:
1. Can't use arithmetic expressions like "s.t+4" directly in Alloy 6 → Must use nested calls to TO/next (ordering) to define the nth successor in Time.
2. Alloy 6 requires all function declarations to have an explicit return type; omitting the return type causes a syntax error.
3. Alloy doesn't support method-like function application with arguments in all contexts; must use relational join and comprehension.
4. When defining orderings in Alloy, prefer util/ordering for total order relations instead of custom functions, and always specify function return types and bodies correctly per Alloy 6 syntax.
5. Always specify a return type in Alloy 6 function declarations to prevent syntax errors related to omitted type information.

