**Current Requirements:**
```
SYSTEM OVERVIEW
===============
The system is a Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) platform supporting multi-user operations with role hierarchy, temporary delegation, clearance-based authorization, and concurrent request processing. The system enforces security constraints such as clearance-based permissions, role delegation and revocation, and preserves safety, liveness, and integrity under concurrent operations. A new Emergency Module is to be integrated, enabling a global/system-wide operational mode and privileges during emergencies, with specific triggering, duration, and exit requirements. Emergency mode is a system-wide state: when Emergency mode is triggered, all active sessions enter Emergency mode together, and no session may remain in or independently return to Normal mode until the Emergency ends. Only one Emergency may be active at a time. Administrators may not initiate or participate in separate concurrent Emergencies or acquire independent emergency privileges through different sessions. Emergency privileges and policy relaxations apply consistently across all sessions and are revoked globally when the system returns to Normal mode. Emergency mode can be triggered exactly once per session by exactly 2 administrators, is active for an abstract fixed duration but may be ended early by the same 2 administrators, and grants special privileges only to the triggering administrators, applied globally across all sessions. While Emergency mode is active, new triggers are blocked. Roles delegated during Emergency mode are revoked upon exit (before the first time step of Normal mode), while roles delegated prior persist as per their policy.

EXISTING-SYSTEM ASSUMPTIONS AND CONSTRAINTS
============================================
- Only exactly 2 administrators may jointly trigger Emergency mode when the system is in Normal mode; each session may only trigger Emergency mode once. Emergency mode entry is permitted only under these conditions.
- Emergency mode is a global/system-wide state and is time-limited by an abstract fixed duration and cannot persist indefinitely; the system must ensure eventual return to Normal mode for all sessions together. Early termination is permitted by the same 2 administrators who triggered Emergency mode.
- For every EmergencyTrigger, endedAt must be set (by either timeout or early termination). The system MUST return to NormalMode for all sessions together no later than the endedAt of the EmergencyTrigger, and restoration must not be delayed past endedAt.
- Emergency mode, once triggered, must persist for at least one time step before transition back to Normal mode is allowed.
- No new Emergency mode triggers are accepted while Emergency mode is active.
- During Emergency mode, request processing order may be bypassed to prioritize high classification requests; this relaxation is temporary and applies system-wide.
- Users may temporarily hold predefined mutually exclusive roles only during Emergency mode. Mutual exclusivity must be symmetric (if A is mutex with B, then B is mutex with A) and paired, and roles cannot be mutex with themselves.
- In Emergency mode, if a user holds two mutually exclusive roles, at least one must have been delegated.
- Only the administrators who triggered Emergency mode hold emergency privileges, defined as an abstract set of permissions, and only for the duration of Emergency mode. No administrator other than the two who triggered Emergency mode may hold emergency privileges at any time. Emergency privileges are assigned and revoked globally across all sessions.
- All roles delegated during Emergency mode must be revoked at the Emergency end, before the first time step of Normal mode. Roles delegated prior to Emergency mode persist until expiry or administrator revocation.
- Administrators who held emergency privileges are required to update their credentials immediately upon exit from Emergency mode before resuming normal operations.
- Self-delegation is prohibited: a user cannot delegate a role to themselves.
- Only directly assigned roles may be delegated; delegation of delegated roles is prohibited.
- Delegation authority is restricted to users with direct assignment of the role.
- The expiry of a delegated role must be strictly after its delegation time (delegatedAt; use only this variable to denote initiation).
- For delegation initiation, only the variable delegatedAt is to be used to mark when a delegation is initiated; do not use InitiatedIn or duringEmergency variables. Whether a delegation is an Emergency-mode delegation is determined by the system mode at the time of delegatedAt.
- For every request, the request processor (the entity processing the request) must be recorded, regardless of whether the request was approved or rejected.
- All administrators have high clearance, but not all high-clearance users are administrators.
