[DIAGNOSIS]:
The error "The name 'EmergencyMode_InitialNormal' cannot be found" at line 214 arises because Alloy 6 requires that predicates and functions be invoked with square brackets, even when they have no parameters (e.g., EmergencyMode_InitialNormal[]). However, the actual error message here indicates that at the point where EmergencyMode_InitialNormal[] is being called inside fact EmergencyModeGlobality, the name EmergencyMode_InitialNormal is not in scope or not defined as a predicate or function. Upon close inspection of the model, EmergencyMode_InitialNormal is declared as a fact, not as a predicate or function. Alloy facts are not invokable; they are constraints that are always globally enforced. Only predicates and functions can be invoked using [] in Alloy. Thus, the error is caused by attempting to "call" a fact as if it were a predicate.

[FIX INTENT]:
Remove all invocations of facts (such as EmergencyMode_InitialNormal[]) as if they were predicates inside the EmergencyModeGlobality fact; facts should not be invoked or referenced explicitly.

[REPAIR INSTRUCTIONS]:
- Step 1: In the fact EmergencyModeGlobality (lines 213-222), remove all lines that "call" facts as if they were predicates (i.e., lines like EmergencyMode_InitialNormal[], EmergencyMode_TriggerUnique[], etc.). Facts are globally enforced and do not need to be invoked.
- Step 2: If you need to modularly compose constraints, refactor these as predicates instead of facts, then invoke them as needed. Otherwise, simply delete these invocations and rely on the global facts.

[RATIONALE]:
This fix conforms to Alloy 6 syntax by distinguishing between facts (which are always on and cannot be called) and predicates (which can be invoked), and avoids the prior failed approach of inserting semicolons or changing quantifier structure. It directly corrects the misuse of fact invocation, which is the real root cause.

[LESSON]:
In Alloy, facts are automatically and globally enforced constraints and should never be "called" or "invoked" inside other blocks; only predicates or functions can be invoked.
