Previous Lessons:
1. In Alloy 6, all function declarations must have an explicit return type to avoid syntax errors, and function application requires square brackets [] for argument lists instead of parentheses ().
2. Alloy 6 requires Boolean-valued functions to specify their return type as Bool and use a single Boolean formula as the body, prohibits multiple top-level quantified expressions joined by "and" in assertion bodies, and mandates curly braces for function bodies to include a return value, not a predicate/Boolean formula.
3. Alloy doesn't support method-like function application with arguments in all contexts; must use relational join and comprehension.
4. - Alloy 6 requires `open` statements to be at the very beginning of the model, before any comments or code lines. Preceding comments can cause syntax errors.
5. Always specify a return type in Alloy 6 function declarations to prevent syntax errors related to omitted type information.

