=== Systematic Unsatisfiability Analysis ===
[Provide analysis for each UNSAT predicate]

1. baseline
- The baseline predicate is UNSAT, meaning the current global facts (before any extra requirement predicates) are unsatisfiable. This is a critical failure: no instance of the system can exist as modeled.
- Key constraints in play:
  - EmergencyTriggerOccursOnce fact: exactly one EmergencyTrigger per execution
  - EachUserAssignedAtLeastOneRole: every user must always have at least one direct role assignment
  - AdminsHaveHighClearance: all administrators have high clearance
  - EmergencyModeGlobality (composed facts): strongly constrains mode transitions, timing, and admin privileges
  - DelegationValidity: restricts delegation to direct assignments, prohibits self-delegation, etc.
  - MutexRolePair constraints: symmetric, paired, no self-mutex
- Likely blocking points:
  - EmergencyTriggerOccursOnce may force an EmergencyTrigger in all executions, but global facts may inadvertently prevent its creation given current bounds/constraints on Administrator, User, or State.
  - EmergencyModeGlobality's temporal constraints may not be feasible with current finite bounds (e.g., 5 States, only 3 Administrators), especially if EmergencyTrigger requires exactly two admins and they must also be assigned to roles consistent with other facts.
  - DelegationValidity and EachUserAssignedAtLeastOneRole may, in combination, prevent the assignment of roles/delegation needed to enable even a trivial execution if role assignment is not possible due to mutex or clearance constraints.
