3. ** Modeling temporal simultaneity as a concurrency event enables a clean abstraction of "simultaneous" triggers without concrete time intervals. Using separate SystemMode states indexed by Time allows temporal reasoning about mode transitions and properties. Abstracting emergency privileges as distinct Permission subsets clarifies privilege grants and revocation. Temporarily relaxing constraints (mutual exclusivity, FIFO ordering) during Emergency is effectively modeled by conditional facts based on mode. Delegated roles with delegation time and expiry allow temporal scoping and revocation modeling. Assertions and incremental predicates enable stepwise verification of requirements.
4. Explicitly modeling mutual exclusivity constraints as conditionally active based on system mode is critical to avoid contradictory facts causing vacuity or unsatisfiability. Abstracting temporal simultaneity as concurrency events simplifies emergency triggering verification but requires careful handling of concurrency windows. Small scopes can hide subtle concurrency or revocation bugs; increasing scope improves confidence.
5. To require at least one delegation per mode for verification, must add a predicate (not fact) and assert it only for coverage, not as a system constraint.**

